Link the window system rather than open it

GLFW opens libwayland-client, libX11 and the rest at run time and asks each
of them for its symbols by name. That is the right thing for a library that
has to run on a machine which may have either window system, or neither: it
is also exactly what a program with no dynamic loader in it cannot do.

Every one of those lookups names a symbol that is known when this is
compiled, so each becomes the symbol itself and the libraries are linked.
What is left of loading a module is the question of whether it is there,
and the answer is a list: a name this build linked is there, anything else
is not -- which is the same answer the loader used to give on a machine
without it, and which matters because GLFW asks for libGL and libGLESv2 to
resolve GL entry points, and then falls back to eglGetProcAddress, which is
what a build like this wants it to do.

